  ],
  "text": "A battery does not store electricity. It stores a chemical reaction that is waiting to happen. Two materials that would react if they touched, kept apart on purpose. Between them sits a substance that lets the ions cross but blocks the electrons. So the electrons cannot take the short way. They have to go around the outside. That detour is the current, and everything you plug in sits along it. A rechargeable one just runs the same reaction backwards. But it never runs perfectly backwards. Each cycle leaves small permanent changes in the structure. Those changes add up, and that is exactly what capacity fade is. Heat speeds it up. So does sitting at a full charge, and running it flat. You are not filling a tank. You are bending something back into shape, and it remembers. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
